      "body": "UFN Intelligence has identified a recurring authentication protocol used by Ghost patrol and command vessels when an unfamiliar ship enters controlled space.\n\nGhost ships of certain command or patrol classes are expected to initiate contact with new arrivals. The first exchange is normally a numeric code challenge. A correct numeric response establishes basic network compatibility but does not guarantee trust.\n\nAfter the numeric exchange, Ghost operators commonly transmit an authentication phrase. These phrases are informal by design: they sound like ordinary Ghost chatter, but function as a second confirmation layer. A correct phrase response is usually enough to continue passing as an accepted contact.\n\nIf the responding vessel behaves unusually, carries inconsistent signals, or gives answers too slowly, the Ghost operator may escalate to a question challenge. These follow-up questions are used to test whether the crew understands Ghost assumptions, priorities, and operating culture.\n\nRecommended response sequence:\n\n1. Answer the numeric code first.\n2. Answer the phrase challenge second.\n3. Keep question-response material ready in case the Ghost operator probes further.\n\nOperational warning:\n\nGhost authentication is social as well as technical. Correct codes can open the channel, but suspicious behaviour may still trigger further challenges or hostile action."
